      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "**Project Overview**\n",
        "\n",
        "This project aims to answer a fatefully impactful, question: When the Pacific Island nation of Tuvalu be submerged due to global sea-level rise driven by the melting of Greenland and Antarctic ice sheets. Tuvalu is a small, low-lying island nation, with an average elevation of only just 2 meters above sea level. Any increase in sea level no matter the magnitude will have severe consequences upon the populus, but for our investigation we will look to find when will Tuvalu be fully submerged.\n",
        "\n",
        "Our focus on the Greenland Ice Sheet is strategic. It represents one of the largest contributors to global sea-level rise, and extensive,\n",
        "Greenland and Antarctica are the 2 main water ice deposits on Earth and have readily available high-quality observational data from initiatives like the Ice Sheet Mass Balance Inter-comparison Exercise (IMBIE). While other ice deposits and thermal expansion also contribute to sea-level rise, isolating these 2 ice sheets allows for a focused and robust modeling exercise based on a well-documented dataset. The dataset provides cumulative mass balance data in gigatonnes (Gt), which can be directly converted into an equivalent sea-level rise.\n",
        "\n",
        "The initial phase involved understanding the nature of the ice melt data and how best to model its progression over extended periods. We anticipated a non-linear trend, given the complex dynamics of ice melt, leading us to consider advanced regression techniques. The primary challenge was to project historical and current melt rates far into the future, providing a probabilistic estimate of when our defined threshold would be crossed. This project serves as a compelling case study of applying Bayesian statistical modeling to real-world climate data for long-term predictions.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n"
      ],
