    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "The data was in units of mm, rather than Gt of ice melted, having already been converted from Gt of ice to the corresponding mm of sea level rise. This conversion was likely done using a formula along these lines.\n",
        "\n",
        "Surface area of the world's oceans: $3.618×10^8 \\text{ km}^2$\n",
        "\n",
        "Volume = Area x Height\n",
        "\n",
        "Height (1mm) = $10^{-6} \\text{ km}$\n",
        "\n",
        "Volume (km$^3$) = $3.618×10^8 \\times 10^{-6} = 316.8 \\text{ km}^3$\n",
        "\n",
        "Assuming density of water and ice are pretty much the same, 1 km$^3$ = 1 Gt, and therefore 316.8 Gt of ice melting is equal to 1mm of sea level rise globally.\n",
        "\n",
        "Even if density is factored in, e.g. density of sea water = 1027 kg/m$^3$, density of pure water = 1000 kg/m$^3$ and density of glacier ice = 916.7 kg/m$^3$, the conversions are still relatively close to this 316.8 number."
      ],
      "metadata": {
